---
import GuideControls from "./GuideControls.astro";
import { github } from "../data/tools";
const { tool: t } = Astro.props;
const trick = t.slug === "trickplay-generator",
  cat = t.slug === "cataloger";
const pkg = trick ? "pillow" : cat ? "XlsxWriter" : "";
const pkgName = trick ? "Pillow" : pkg;
const aptPackages = `python3 ffmpeg${trick ? " python3-pil" : cat ? " python3-xlsxwriter" : ""}`;
const dnfPackages = `python3 ffmpeg${trick ? " python3-pillow" : cat ? " python3-XlsxWriter" : ""}`;
const rpmFusion =
  "sudo dnf install https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/free/fedora/rpmfusion-free-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm";
const ffmpegBuildCommands = [
  "mkdir -p ffmpeg/bin",
  "curl -L -o ffmpeg.tar.xz https://johnvansickle.com/ffmpeg/releases/ffmpeg-release-amd64-static.tar.xz",
  "tar -xf ffmpeg.tar.xz --strip-components=1 -C ffmpeg/bin --wildcards '*/ffmpeg' '*/ffprobe'",
  "rm ffmpeg.tar.xz",
  "ffmpeg/bin/ffprobe -version",
].join("\n");
---

<article class="wrap prose product-document">
  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
  <p>{t.requirements}</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Keep this application in its own writable folder.</li>
    <li>Read access to all source media folders is required.</li>
    <li>
      {trick
        ? "Write access beside source videos is required for trickplay output."
        : cat
          ? "The application writes its catalog under data/ and reports under reports/ in the project folder."
          : "Write access to the selected output folder is required for copied videos and manifests."}
    </li>
    <li>Original videos are read without modification.</li>
  </ul>
  <nav class="doc-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <strong>In this guide</strong>
    <a href="#requirements">Requirements</a>
    <a href="#source">Get the source</a>
    <a href="#dependencies">Install dependencies</a>
    <a href="#first-run">Configure and run</a>
    <a href="#continue">Stop and continue</a>
    {trick && <a href="#unattended">Unattended operation</a>}
    <a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>
  </nav>
  <GuideControls />
  <h2 id="source">1. Get the source</h2>
  <p>
    Open the{" "}
    <a href={`${github}${t.repo}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      project repository
    </a>
    , choose Code → Download ZIP, then extract it into a permanent, writable
    folder. Alternatively, use Git:
  </p>
  <pre
    data-copy
  ><code>{`git clone ${github}${t.repo}.git\ncd ${t.repo}`}</code></pre>
  <h2 id="dependencies">2. Install dependencies</h2>
  <div data-platform="windows">
    <h3>Windows</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Install{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.python.org/downloads/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Python 3.10 or newer
        </a>
        . Enable “Add python.exe to PATH”.
      </li>
      <li>
        Download a Windows FFmpeg build from{" "}
        <a
          href="https://www.gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Gyan’s FFmpeg builds
        </a>
        . Extract the binaries into the layout below. The tool does not download
        FFmpeg for you.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>{`${t.repo}/\n  main.py\n  ffmpeg/\n    bin/\n      ffprobe.exe${trick?'\n      ffmpeg.exe':''}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Windows uses the project’s FFmpeg folder; it does not search PATH for
      these binaries.
    </p>
    {pkg && (
      <>
        <p>
          {cat
            ? "For Excel reports only, install XlsxWriter. Skip this if you only need JSON and TXT."
            : "Install Pillow to assemble the preview images."}
        </p>
        <pre data-copy><code>{`py -3 -m pip install ${pkg}`}</code></pre>
      </>
    )}
    {trick && (
      <p>
        For an NVIDIA graphics card, keep the graphics driver up to date.
        Nothing else is needed. Without a GPU everything runs on the CPU.
      </p>
    )}
    <p>From the project folder, start the program:</p>
    <pre data-copy><code>py -3 main.py</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div data-platform="linux">
    <h3>Linux: packages from your distribution</h3>
    <p>
      The simplest way.{" "}
      {cat ? "The XlsxWriter package is only needed for Excel reports." : ""}
      {trick ? "Pillow comes from the distribution too." : ""}
      {!pkg ? "No Python package beyond Python itself is needed." : ""}
    </p>
    <p class="code-label">Debian, Ubuntu</p>
    <pre data-copy><code>{`sudo apt install ${aptPackages}`}</code></pre>
    <p class="code-label">Fedora</p>
    <p>
      Fedora’s own <code>ffmpeg-free</code> leaves out H.264 and HEVC, which
      most videos use. Enable RPM Fusion and install its FFmpeg instead:
    </p>
    <pre data-copy><code>{rpmFusion}</code></pre>
    <pre
      data-copy
    ><code>{`sudo dnf install --allowerasing ${dnfPackages}`}</code></pre>
    <p>From the project folder, start the program:</p>
    <pre data-copy><code>python3 main.py</code></pre>
    {pkg && (
      <>
        <h3>Linux: virtual environment</h3>
        <p>
          Use this instead of the packages above if you prefer to keep {pkgName}{" "}
          out of the system Python.
        </p>
        <p class="code-label">Debian, Ubuntu</p>
        <pre
          data-copy
        ><code>sudo apt install python3 python3-venv ffmpeg</code></pre>
        <p class="code-label">Fedora</p>
        <p>
          Enable RPM Fusion as above first; Fedora’s own{" "}
          <code>ffmpeg-free</code> leaves out H.264 and HEVC.
        </p>
        <pre
          data-copy
        ><code>sudo dnf install --allowerasing python3 ffmpeg</code></pre>
        <p>
          Then, in the project folder, create the environment and install{" "}
          {pkgName}:
        </p>
        <pre
          data-copy
        ><code>{`python3 -m venv .venv\n.venv/bin/pip install ${pkg}`}</code></pre>
        <p>Start it with the interpreter from the environment:</p>
        <pre data-copy><code>.venv/bin/python main.py</code></pre>
      </>
    )}
    {trick && (
      <p>
        For an NVIDIA graphics card, install the proprietary driver. The{" "}
        <code>nvidia-smi</code> command must work.
      </p>
    )}
    <details class="guide-details">
      <summary>Optional: your own static FFmpeg build</summary>
      <p>
        The system FFmpeg is enough. If you want a specific build instead,
        download a static one into the ffmpeg/bin folder next to main.py. A
        build there takes precedence over the system binaries;{" "}
        {trick ? "ffmpeg and ffprobe" : "ffprobe"} must be in it. The download
        commands are the same on Debian, Ubuntu and Fedora. On a 64-bit ARM
        machine such as a Raspberry Pi, use <code>arm64</code> instead of{" "}
        <code>amd64</code> in the download address.
      </p>
      <p>First install curl and xz:</p>
      <p class="code-label">Debian, Ubuntu</p>
      <pre data-copy><code>sudo apt install curl xz-utils</code></pre>
      <p class="code-label">Fedora</p>
      <pre data-copy><code>sudo dnf install curl xz</code></pre>
      <p>
        Then, from the project folder (the one that holds main.py), download the
        build:
      </p>
      <pre data-copy><code>{ffmpegBuildCommands}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <p>
      More detail is in the{" "}
      <a
        href={`${github}${t.repo}/blob/main/README.md`}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >
        project README
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </div>
  <h2 id="first-run">3. Configure and run</h2>
  <ol>
    {t.steps.map((s) => (
      <li>{s}</li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p>
    The menus are in English. Slovenian is included and can be chosen under
    Configuration → Language.
  </p>
  {trick && (
    <div class="note" role="note">
      <h3>Jellyfin setup</h3>
      <p>
        Enable “Save trickplay images next to media” in the Jellyfin library
        settings. The generator writes Movie.trickplay folders beside the source
        video. It needs write access there. Check your own hardware with the
        benchmark and self-test.
      </p>
    </div>
  )}
  <div class="note" role="note">
    <h3>What a successful first run produces</h3>
    <p>
      {trick
        ? "A .trickplay folder beside each successfully processed video, containing JPEG sprite sheets. Check it with Verify."
        : cat
          ? "A local catalog and the report formats you selected under reports/, grouped by date."
          : "An output library containing copies of your selected videos, with manifests tracking build progress."}
    </p>
  </div>
  <h2 id="continue">4. Stop and continue</h2>
  <p>
    {trick
      ? "During generation, press Ctrl+C once to let active videos finish; a second press stops immediately. Choose Generate / Resume later. Valid completed outputs are skipped."
      : cat
        ? "Press Ctrl+C once to stop a scan gracefully. Run a normal scan again to reuse unchanged metadata."
        : "Press Ctrl+C once to stop gracefully. Build / Resume continues the copy plan later."}
  </p>
  {trick && (
    <>
      <h2 id="unattended">Unattended operation</h2>
      <p>
        Configure media folders and settings in the menu first. These actions
        run without interactive questions:
      </p>
      {["windows", "linux"].map((platform) => (
        <div data-platform={platform}>
          <h3>{platform === "windows" ? "Windows" : "Linux"}</h3>
          <pre
            data-copy
          ><code>{["--generate --dry-run", "--generate", "--verify", "--self-test"].map(flag => `${platform === "windows" ? "py -3" : "python3"} main.py ${flag}`).join("\n")}</code></pre>
        </div>
      ))}
      <p>
        Use Task Scheduler on Windows or cron / a systemd timer on Linux. The
        dry run plans generation without creating trickplay output. Verify
        checks existing previews without modifying them; the application can
        still write its own logs.
      </p>
    </>
  )}
  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      FFprobe not found: check the project-local folder on Windows, or the
      local/system installation on Linux.
    </li>
    <li>
      Source unavailable: verify that your disk or network share is mounted and
      readable by the user running the tool. On Linux, check a CIFS or NFS share
      outside the program, for example <code>ls /mnt/media</code>. With a
      systemd automount, reading the path triggers the mount.
    </li>
    <li>
      Permission denied: check access to the project folder and relevant output
      folder.
    </li>
    {trick && (
      <li>
        GPU trouble: run the backend compatibility check and benchmark. CPU
        operation is available without a GPU.
      </li>
    )}
  </ul>
  <p>
    Still need help?{" "}
    <a
      href={`${github}${t.repo}/issues`}
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Report an issue on GitHub ↗
    </a>{" "}
    or <a href="#technical">read the technical reference →</a>.
  </p>
</article>
